#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build the docs site from the Markdown pages and the samples.

    scripts/build [--out DIR] [--cli PATH] [--app PATH] [--forge-url URL] [--mirror-url URL]

Pages are `index.md`, `samples/index.md` (the Samples page), `tutorials/*.md`
and `manual/*.md`, each section with an `index.md` of its own. A page other than an index is published
when its metadata block carries `Checked:` (the Blockwright build its steps
were checked against); the others are stubs, listed as not written yet and
never linked. The maintainer's pages in `STANDALONE` (`release-checklist.md`)
are built at the site root, always, outside both sections: no list, menu or
pager names them, and each carries a `noindex` robots meta tag.

An image whose source is an `.ans` or a `.bwp` project
(`![alt](../samples/x/y.ans "2x")`) is drawn by `blockwright-cli render` (a
project as its composite) at the scale its title names (1x when it names
none), as `y.ans-2x.png` beside it, and links to the sample, which is copied
beside the picture. Any other image must be a `.png`, copied as it is; an
image of any other kind fails the build.

Any other relative link names a file in the repo (a `.bwp` or an `.ans` no
page draws), which is copied into the site at the same path.

`<!-- generated: cli-help -->` at the start of a line of its own (in column 0:
not indented, not in a list item or blockquote), outside code, becomes the
output of `blockwright-cli --help`. `<!-- generated: checklist -->` becomes a
table per section of every tutorial and manual page: its
status in `PLAN.md` (the leading words of the Status cell of the row in a table
with a Tutorial or Page column whose name, made a slug, is the page's file name
without its number), the commit its `Checked:` names, and whether that is the
CLI's commit (ready), another one (behind) or none (not written). `<!-- generated: shortcuts -->` becomes
a table per menu of every action `blockwright --list-actions` lists: its name,
its default keys and what it does. The app's `--version` must name the same
Blockwright commit as the CLI's, and the app may take $BW_DOCS_APP_TIMEOUT
seconds (default 120) to answer. `<!-- list: tutorials -->`
and `<!-- list: manual -->` become the list of that section's pages. A marker
is written exactly so: lowercase, one space after `<!--`, after the colon and
before `-->`. Markers are replaced inside Python-Markdown, after it has set
fenced code aside, so a marker is code exactly when Markdown shows it as code.
A marker comment still in a page's HTML fails the build: one in the wrong place
with a message naming the column-0 rule, a misspelled one (any `<!-- generated:`
or `<!-- list:` comment, in any case, that isn't one of the five) with a
message naming the five.

Python-Markdown finds a fenced block only at the left margin. A fence inside a
list item, indented to the item's content (four spaces a level), is set aside
before it looks: the block, less that indent, goes through fenced_code's own
code, so it comes out as a top-level fence does (`<pre><code
class="language-python">`, the text as written), and a marker in it is code.
An item's first paragraph may wrap onto unindented lines. An item line indented
four spaces or more counts only inside another item, and a raw HTML block (as
Python-Markdown's own parser finds one) or a fenced block at the left margin
hides what it holds, so a fence in an indented code block or in raw HTML is
left as written. A fence that still isn't
code fails the build, naming the page and the line: a line of a page's text
outside <pre> and <code> that fenced_code would take for an opening or closing
fence (three or more backticks or tildes, then at most a language or
{attributes}), where a line of the page's Markdown, less its indent, quote
marks and list marker, reads the same. Backticks from escapes or a table cell
aren't a fence. A code span opened by such a fence at the start of a line and
running over lines, which is what Markdown makes of one whose backticks pair
up, is left as text so the check finds it.

Every page carries the site menu (`theme/page.html`'s `$nav`): the home link,
then each section's index and its pages in the order the index lists them,
stubs dimmed and unlinked; the page itself is marked `aria-current="page"` and
its section `here`. A standalone page carries the menu too, with nothing in it
marked. The home page's `<body>` has the class `home-page`, which the
stylesheet's home layout hangs on (not `:has()`, which older browsers drop).

The site also gets `robots.txt`, which lets crawlers fetch everything, and,
when `ROBOTS` is set, a robots meta tag on every page but the standalone ones,
which always carry `noindex`.

Pages link to the forge as `https://git.pumpin.online/...` (FORGE). `--forge-url
URL` puts URL in its place in every href and src of the built site, so a copy
of the site can send downloads to another address of the forge. `--mirror-url
URL` adds a line to every page's footer naming the site's other address. Every
tag of every built page, the template's included, must be one the site uses
(TAGS, ATTRS: nothing that loads, embeds, runs or redirects), its one
stylesheet link the template's own `site.css`, and every href and src the
forge, the mirror, the licence (ALLOWED_EXTERNAL), a mailto: or a plain
relative path; anything else fails the build, so the site works where only
those addresses can be reached. Text in code, in <pre> and in comments is
no link; --forge-url moves the forge's address in text outside comments too, so
a command on a page names the same forge as the page's links. The stylesheet,
`theme/site.css`, loads nothing either: it may use only the at-rules and
functions in CSS_AT_RULES and CSS_FUNCTIONS (no @import, @font-face, url() or
image-set()) and no backslash, which could spell one as a CSS escape, so it
names no address for --forge-url to move.

The build fails on a link or image that doesn't resolve, a `#fragment` the
target page has no id for, a link to a stub, a path that leaves the repo or
resolves into `.git/` or `site/`, a missing sample, a sample the CLI can't
render, or an image that is neither a sample nor a `.png`.

The output directory is cleared only when it is empty or is a site this script
built: it writes `.blockwright-docs-manifest` (every file it wrote) into the
site, and refuses a directory without one, or with a file the manifest doesn't
list. It refuses
any output inside the repo other than `site/`, and any that contains the repo.
"""

import argparse
import html
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import unicodedata
from pathlib import Path
from string import Template
from urllib.parse import unquote

try:
    import markdown
    from markdown.extensions.md_in_html import HTMLExtractorExtra
    from markdown.inlinepatterns import InlineProcessor
    from markdown.preprocessors import Preprocessor
    from markdown.util import AtomicString
except ImportError:
    sys.exit("build: needs Python-Markdown (python-markdown on Arch, python3-markdown on Debian)")

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
SECTIONS = {"tutorials": "Tutorials", "manual": "Manual"}
# Pages of their own, each a folder's index.md: listed in the menu after the
# home link, before the sections.
TOP_PAGES = {"samples": "Samples"}
# The maintainer's pages at the site root: built always, linked from no list,
# menu or pager, and kept out of search engines (PLAN.md §6).
STANDALONE = ("release-checklist.md",)
SITE_NAME = "Blockwright"
SCALE_RE = re.compile(r"^([1-8])x$")
# Images drawn by blockwright-cli render, and images copied as they are.
SAMPLE_TYPES = (".ans", ".bwp")
IMAGE_TYPES = (".png",)
# The robots meta tag every page carries; "" for none. The site has its own
# domain and is meant to be indexed (PLAN.md §7).
ROBOTS = ""
# Written into the site as robots.txt: crawlers may fetch everything.
ROBOTS_TXT = "User-agent: *\nAllow: /\n"
# Written into the site; names every file the build wrote.
MANIFEST = ".blockwright-docs-manifest"
# The forge as pages write it; --forge-url puts another address in its place.
FORGE = "https://git.pumpin.online"
# The only links out of the site besides the forge and the mirror.
ALLOWED_EXTERNAL = ("https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",)
# An address --forge-url and --mirror-url take: http or https, a host, an
# optional port and a path of letters, digits, / . _ ~ % and -, nothing else
# (no character that needs escaping in a URL list or in CSS).
URL_RE = re.compile(r"^https?://[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9.-]*[A-Za-z0-9])?(?::\d{1,5})?(?:/[A-Za-z0-9._~%/-]*)?$")
ABSOLUTE_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:https?:)?//", re.I)
# A character a reader sees as a space: Python's \s without U+000B, U+001C to
# U+001F and U+0085, control characters that show as nothing.
SPACE = r"[^\S\x0b\x1c-\x1f\x85]"
# The forge's address in text, with the run of text after it up to a space or a
# <. Whether it moves is forge_ends()'s to say.
FORGE_TEXT_RE = re.compile(re.escape(FORGE) + r"(?=((?:(?!" + SPACE + r")[^<])*))")
# What a URL parser drops from a host name (soft hyphen, zero-width and joining
# characters, variation selectors and the like).
IGNORED_RE = re.compile("[\u00ad\u034f\u17b4\u17b5\u180b-\u180f\u200b-\u200d\u2060-\u206f\ufe00-\ufe0f\ufeff"
                        "\U0001bca0-\U0001bca3\U0001d173-\U0001d17a\U000e0100-\U000e01ef]")
# HTML as the link pass reads it: a comment (closed as HTML closes one, at
# <!--> and <!---> too), a tag, or the start of a tag it can't read. A tag is
# its name and its attributes, each a name with an optional value in double,
# single or no quotes, so a quoted value may hold < > = and attribute names
# without being taken for more of the tag. Whitespace is HTML's: ASCII only.
ATTR = r"""([ \t\n\f\r]+)([^ \t\n\f\r"'<>/=]+)(?:([ \t\n\f\r]*=[ \t\n\f\r]*)(?:"([^"]*)"|'([^']*)'|([^ \t\n\f\r"'=<>`]+)))?"""
TAG_RE = re.compile(r"<!--(?:>|->|.*?--!?>)|<!--"  # a comment, or one left open
                    r"|<![^>]*>|<\?[^>]*>|</(?![A-Za-z])[^>]*>"  # bogus comments: to the first >
                    r"|</?(?:[A-Za-z][^ \t\n\f\r/>]*(?:" + ATTR + r""")*[ \t\n\f\r]*/?>)?""", re.S)
ATTR_RE = re.compile(ATTR, re.S)
# The tags and attributes a built page may hold: what Python-Markdown's
# extensions and the template write, and nothing that loads, embeds, runs or
# redirects. Only href and src name an address.
TAGS = set("""a abbr b blockquote body br caption code col colgroup dd del details div dl dt em figcaption figure
    footer h1 h2 h3 h4 h5 h6 head header hr html i img ins kbd li link main mark meta nav ol p pre q s section
    small span strong sub summary sup table tbody td tfoot th thead title tr u ul""".split())
ATTRS = {"*": {"class", "id", "title", "lang", "dir"},
         "a": {"href", "rel", "aria-current"}, "img": {"src", "alt", "width", "height"}, "link": {"href", "rel"},
         "meta": {"charset", "name", "content"}, "nav": {"aria-label"}, "ol": {"start"}, "details": {"open"},
         "td": {"style", "colspan", "rowspan"}, "th": {"style", "colspan", "rowspan"}}
ADDRESS_ATTRS = {"href", "src"}
# The one style a page may carry: a table column's alignment.
STYLE_RE = re.compile(r"^text-align: ?(?:left|right|center);?$")
# What theme/site.css may use: nothing that loads, so no @import, @font-face
# or @namespace, and no url(), image(), image-set() or any other function that
# takes an address. A backslash is refused too: a CSS escape can spell url(.
CSS_AT_RULES = {"media", "supports", "keyframes"}
CSS_FUNCTIONS = {"var", "calc", "min", "max", "clamp", "minmax", "repeat", "inset", "steps",
                 "not", "is", "where", "has", "selector"}
CSS_AT_RE = re.compile(r"@(-?-?[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)")
CSS_FUNCTION_RE = re.compile(r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9_@-])(-?-?[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)\(")
EXTERNAL_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*:|^//", re.I)
MARKER_RE = re.compile(r"^<!-- (generated|list): ([a-z-]+) -->[ \t]*$")
# The markers a page may use, exactly as written.
MARKERS = ("<!-- generated: cli-help -->", "<!-- generated: shortcuts -->", "<!-- generated: checklist -->",
           "<!-- list: tutorials -->", "<!-- list: manual -->")
# The commit in a version or a Checked: line: "Blockwright 0.1.0 (30c37b9)".
COMMIT_RE = re.compile(r"\(([^()\s]+)\)\s*$")
# How long the app may take to answer --version or --list-actions before the
# build gives up, in seconds; $BW_DOCS_APP_TIMEOUT sets another.
APP_TIMEOUT = 120
# A marker, or something meant as one, left in a page's HTML: the comment up to
# its close when it has one.
LEFT_MARKER_RE = re.compile(r"<!--\s*(?:generated|list)\s*:(?:[^>]*-->)?", re.I)
# Python-Markdown's fenced_code_block preprocessor runs at 25 and html_block at
# 20; markers are replaced between the two.
MARKER_PRIORITY = 22
# A fence inside a list item is set aside before fenced_code_block (25) looks
# for fences, which it finds only at the left margin; after normalize_whitespace
# (30), so tabs are spaces and blank lines empty, as fenced_code sees them.
# Before html_block (20), since md_in_html gives a block marked markdown="1"
# straight to the block parser.
NESTED_FENCE_PRIORITY = 27
# Where Python-Markdown registers its backtick (code span) pattern; FenceSpans
# takes its place there.
BACKTICK_PRIORITY = 190
# A line that opens or closes a fence: its indent, the fence and the rest.
FENCE_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"^( *)(`{3,}|~{3,})(.*)$")
# A list item's first line, as Python-Markdown reads one: its indent, then a
# bullet or a number and a full stop, and a space.
LIST_ITEM_RE = re.compile(r"^( *)(?:[*+-]|\d+\.)[ ]+\S")
# Lines that end a block of Markdown wherever they are in it, so the next line
# starts one: a hash heading and a rule (Python-Markdown's HashHeaderProcessor
# and HRProcessor), and a setext heading's underline when it is the block's
# second line (SetextHeaderProcessor). Each is matched less the block's indent.
HASH_HEADING_RE = re.compile(r"#")
RULE_RE = re.compile(r"[ ]{0,3}(?=(?P<run>(-+[ ]{0,2}){3,}|(_+[ ]{0,2}){3,}|(\*+[ ]{0,2}){3,}))(?P=run)[ ]*$")
SETEXT_RE = re.compile(r"(?:=+|-+)[ ]*$")
# What the fence check reads past in a page's HTML: a comment, and a <pre> or
# <code> with all it holds.
CODE_RE = re.compile(r"<!--.*?-->|<pre(?=[ \t\n\f\r>]).*?</pre>|<code(?=[ \t\n\f\r>]).*?</code>", re.S | re.I)
# A line of a page's text that reads as an opening or closing fence: three or
# more backticks or tildes, then any info string without the fence's own
# character, whether or not fenced_code_block takes it (it refuses an info
# string such as "shell session", and the fence is then shown as text).
STRAY_FENCE_RE = re.compile(r"`{3,}[^`]*|~{3,}[^~]*")
# What comes before a line's text in Markdown: its indent, quote marks and list markers.
SOURCE_PREFIX_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:[ \t]*(?:>|[*+-][ \t]|\d+\.[ \t]))*[ \t]*")


class BuildError(Exception):
    pass


def find_cli(explicit):
    # One that was asked for is used or refused, never passed over.
    for asked, how in [(explicit, "--cli"), (os.environ.get("BLOCKWRIGHT_CLI"), "BLOCKWRIGHT_CLI")]:
        if asked:
            found = shutil.which(asked)
            if not found:
                raise BuildError(f"{how}: {asked} isn't an executable")
            return found
    for name in ["blockwright-cli-dev", "blockwright-cli"]:
        if shutil.which(name):
            return shutil.which(name)
    raise BuildError("no blockwright-cli: pass --cli or set BLOCKWRIGHT_CLI")


def find_app(explicit, cli):
    """The Blockwright app (the window's binary) that <!-- generated: shortcuts -->
    runs, or None when there is none. One asked for is used or refused, as for
    --cli; else the app installed beside the CLI (blockwright-dev beside
    blockwright-cli-dev, blockwright beside blockwright-cli), else
    blockwright-dev or blockwright on PATH."""
    for asked, how in [(explicit, "--app"), (os.environ.get("BLOCKWRIGHT_APP"), "BLOCKWRIGHT_APP")]:
        if asked:
            found = shutil.which(asked)
            if not found:
                raise BuildError(f"{how}: {asked} isn't an executable")
            return found
    cli = Path(cli)
    if "blockwright-cli" in cli.name:
        beside = shutil.which(str(cli.with_name(cli.name.replace("blockwright-cli", "blockwright", 1))))
        if beside:
            return beside
    for name in ["blockwright-dev", "blockwright"]:
        if shutil.which(name):
            return shutil.which(name)
    return None


def run(cmd):
    r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise BuildError(f"{' '.join(map(str, cmd))} exited {r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()}")
    return r.stdout


def png_size(path):
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            head = f.read(24)
    except OSError as e:
        raise BuildError(f"{path}: the render wrote no picture ({e.strerror})")
    if head[:8] != b"\x89PNG\r\n\x1a\n":
        raise BuildError(f"{path} is not a PNG")
    return struct.unpack(">II", head[16:24])


def in_repo(rel):
    """rel (a path from ROOT) as a normalised Path, or None if it leaves the repo."""
    path = Path(os.path.normpath(rel))
    if path.is_absolute() or path.parts[:1] == ("..",):
        return None
    if not (ROOT / path).resolve().is_relative_to(ROOT):
        return None  # a symlink out of the repo
    return path


def refused(target):
    """Why the site can't carry target (a path from in_repo), or None. Checked on
    the resolved path, so a symlink into .git/ or site/ is refused too."""
    real = (ROOT / target).resolve().relative_to(ROOT)
    if not real.parts or real.parts[0] in (".git", "site"):
        return "not a file the site can carry"
    return None


def unknown_marker(page, found):
    return (f"{page.rel}: {found} is not a marker the build knows; the markers are "
            f"{', '.join(MARKERS)}, written exactly so (lowercase, single spaces as shown)")


class Markers(Preprocessor):
    """Replaces each marker line Markdown hasn't set aside as code with the HTML
    replace(kind, name) returns, stashed so Markdown passes it through as is."""

    def __init__(self, md, replace):
        super().__init__(md)
        self.replace = replace

    def run(self, lines):
        out = []
        for line in lines:
            m = MARKER_RE.match(line)
            if m:
                out += ["", self.md.htmlStash.store(self.replace(m.group(1), m.group(2))), ""]
            else:
                out.append(line)
        return out


class NestedFences(Preprocessor):
    """Sets aside each fenced block in a list item, at the item's content indent
    (four spaces a level), the way fenced_code sets aside one at the left margin:
    the block less that indent goes through fenced_code's own run(), and its
    placeholder goes back at the indent, between blank lines, so it stays in the
    item. A fence at the left margin is left to fenced_code, and a raw HTML
    block to html_block, with all they hold; a fence at any other indent, one
    not in a list item (an indented code block's text among them), and one that
    isn't closed at its own indent are left as they are, for the stray fence
    check. So is one inside an element md_in_html parses as span Markdown or
    leaves raw, which holds no list, and one in a raw HTML block, as md_in_html
    finds them (markdown_edges). A fence in a list item whose list is the child
    of an element md_in_html parses block Markdown in and the block parser
    takes for a list item or a list (<li markdown="block">, <ol markdown="1">)
    is left as it is too, and noted in refused: Python-Markdown puts the block
    beside the item, outside every <li>."""

    def run(self, lines):
        fenced = self.md.preprocessors["fenced_code_block"]
        # The lines a closed fence at the left margin or a raw HTML block holds
        # are None in seen: they aren't looked at, and in_item doesn't read past one.
        seen = list(lines)
        i = 0
        while i < len(lines):
            m = FENCE_LINE_RE.match(lines[i])
            end = self.closing(lines, i, 0, m.group(2)) if m and not m.group(1) else None
            if end is None:
                i += 1
            else:
                seen[i:end + 1] = [None] * (end + 1 - i)
                i = end + 1
        text = ["" if line is None else line for line in seen]
        edges, inline, raw, items = markdown_edges(text, self.md)
        self.edges = {n - 1 for n in edges}
        self.inline = {n - 1 for n in inline}
        self.items = {n - 1 for n in items}
        for n in raw:
            seen[n - 1] = None
        self.refused = []
        out = []
        i = 0
        while i < len(lines):
            m = FENCE_LINE_RE.match(lines[i]) if seen[i] is not None and i not in self.inline else None
            end = self.closing(lines, i, len(m.group(1)), m.group(2)) if m else None
            if end is None:
                out.append(lines[i])
                i += 1
                continue
            indent = len(m.group(1))
            if indent and not indent % 4 and self.in_item(seen, i, indent):
                if i in self.items:
                    self.refused.append(lines[i].strip())
                    out.append(lines[i])
                    i += 1
                    continue
                got = fenced.run([line[indent:] for line in lines[i:end + 1]])
                if len(got) == 3 and not got[0] and not got[2]:
                    out += ["", " " * indent + got[1], ""]
                    i = end + 1
                    continue
            out.append(lines[i])
            i += 1
        return out

    @staticmethod
    def closing(lines, start, indent, fence):
        """The index of the line closing the fence opened at start (the same
        fence at the same indent), or None when it isn't closed or a line before
        the close is indented less than the fence."""
        close = re.compile(" " * indent + re.escape(fence) + "[ ]*$")
        for j in range(start + 1, len(lines)):
            if close.match(lines[j]):
                return j
            if lines[j] and not lines[j].startswith(" " * indent):
                return None
        return None

    def in_item(self, lines, start, indent):
        """Whether the line at start, indented indent (four spaces a level), is
        in a list item's content: the last line before it indented less is in a
        block (lines with no blank one between) whose nearest list item's first
        line above it is one level out, or whose first line is at the same
        indent and in an item's content. The lines between are the item's lazy
        continuation, which Python-Markdown puts in the item. An item line
        indented four spaces or more must itself be in an item's content, or it
        is an indented code block's text. A line that is None (code or raw HTML)
        ends the search, and so does a hash heading or a rule between the item
        line and the last line indented less than start, at the item's level or
        less: the list ends there (HashHeaderProcessor and HRProcessor split the
        block where it is), so what follows is in no item."""
        j = start - 1
        while j >= 0 and lines[j] is not None and (not lines[j] or lines[j].startswith(" " * indent)):
            j -= 1
        if j < 0 or lines[j] is None:
            return False
        last = j
        while j >= 0 and lines[j]:
            m = LIST_ITEM_RE.match(lines[j])
            if m and len(m.group(1)) < indent and self.starts_item(lines, j):
                level = len(m.group(1))
                if any(self.ends_block(lines[n], level - level % 4) for n in range(j + 1, last + 1)):
                    return False
                return indent - 4 <= level and (level < 4 or self.in_item(lines, j, level - level % 4))
            j -= 1
        top = len(lines[j + 1]) - len(lines[j + 1].lstrip(" "))
        return lines[j:j + 1] != [None] and top - top % 4 == indent and self.in_item(lines, j + 1, indent)

    @staticmethod
    def ends_block(line, base):
        """Whether line, less up to base spaces of indent, is a hash heading or a
        rule, which end a block of Markdown where they are."""
        text = line[min(base, len(line) - len(line.lstrip(" "))):]
        return bool(HASH_HEADING_RE.match(text) or RULE_RE.match(text))

    def starts_item(self, lines, k):
        """Whether the list item line at k is a list item to Python-Markdown,
        which starts a list only at the start of a block (lines with no blank
        one between): the block's first line, or the first line after a hash
        heading, a rule, a setext heading's underline or an md_in_html edge
        (markdown_edges), which end a block where they are, must itself be a
        list item, indented less than four spaces more than the block, and not
        a rule or a setext heading. Otherwise the line at k is a paragraph's
        text, as is one after "Text:" with no blank line between. No line inside
        an element md_in_html parses as span Markdown or leaves raw starts a
        block, whatever comes before it: its text is inline content."""
        s = k
        while s > 0 and lines[s - 1]:
            s -= 1
        top = len(lines[s]) - len(lines[s].lstrip(" "))
        base = top - top % 4

        def text(n):
            line = lines[n]
            return line[min(base, len(line) - len(line.lstrip(" "))):]

        first = s
        for n in range(s, k + 1):
            if (n == first + 1 and SETEXT_RE.match(text(n)) or HASH_HEADING_RE.match(text(n))
                    or RULE_RE.match(text(n)) or n in self.edges):
                first = n + 1
        if first > k or first in self.inline or not LIST_ITEM_RE.match(lines[first]):
            return False
        lead = len(text(first)) - len(text(first).lstrip(" "))
        under = first + 1 < len(lines) and lines[first + 1] and SETEXT_RE.match(text(first + 1))
        return lead < 4 and not under


class MarkdownEdges(HTMLExtractorExtra):
    """md_in_html's own parser, noting where it splits the text into raw HTML
    and Markdown, every line counted from 1, all from its own state as it goes:

    - edges, the lines after which it starts a block of Markdown: a start tag
      it parses block Markdown in (a block tag marked markdown="1", or a block
      or span tag marked "block", inside no element it parses as span Markdown
      or not at all), and the end tag closing the outermost element it parses
      Markdown in, which it stashes between blank lines. Each only when the tag
      is its line's text, at the left margin. The </p> md_in_html implies when a
      block tag opens inside an open <p> isn't an end tag in the source, and the
      tag opening works out its state with the <p> still open, so it ends no
      block.
    - inline, the lines of each element it parses as span Markdown or leaves
      raw inside an element it parses Markdown in, from the start tag's line to
      the end tag's: everything such an element holds is span or raw too, so no
      line there starts a block, after a blank line or not.
    - raw, the lines of each raw HTML block it sets aside outside every element
      it parses Markdown in: one its fallback (html_block's parser) opens,
      wherever a start tag opens it, at the start of a line or after an end tag
      on the same line (the tail of a raw block or of a Markdown element), from
      the start tag's line to the end tag's, and a comment, declaration or
      empty block tag stashed as a block of its own (inside an element it
      parses block Markdown in too, where it is a block between blank lines).
      So is an outermost element marked as Markdown that doesn't start a block
      of the text it hands on (after a space or text following an end tag on
      its line, or indented, or left open at the end of the text after an
      empty tag, comment or the like held back from a tail, which
      md_in_html's close() writes out straight before it):
      MarkdownInHtmlProcessor takes an element
      only at the start of a block, so the element is written out as it is,
      its content unparsed. Whether it starts one is read from the text where
      md_in_html stashes the element, at its end tag or at the end of the text,
      not where it opens.
    - items, the lines whose innermost element it parses Markdown in is one it
      parses block Markdown in whose tag the block parser's ListIndentProcessor
      takes for a list item or a list (item_types, its ITEM_TYPES and
      LIST_TYPES: <li markdown="block">, <ol markdown="1">, <ul markdown="1">).
      A list in an item element is that item's child, and ListIndentProcessor
      puts a block indented under one of the list's items beside the item, in
      the list; a list in a list element adds its items to that element, and a
      block indented under one is an indented code block beside the item. Either
      way it is in no <li>."""

    def reset(self):
        super().reset()
        self.edges = set()
        self.inline = set()
        self.raw = set()
        self.span = None  # (line, depth) of the open outermost span or raw element
        self.opened = None  # line of the open raw block
        self.outer = None  # line of the open outermost element marked as Markdown
        self.ending = False
        self.starting = False
        self.tops = []  # (line, whether the innermost Markdown element is an item) after each change

    def whole_line(self, text):
        line, offset = self.getpos()
        return offset == 0 and self.lines[line - 1].rstrip(" ") == text

    def note_top(self):
        item = bool(self.mdstack) and self.mdstate[-1] == "block" and self.mdstack[-1] in self.item_types
        if not self.tops or self.tops[-1][1] != item:
            self.tops.append((self.getpos()[0], item))

    def stashed_in_block(self):
        """Whether the element md_in_html has just stashed (its placeholder and a
        blank line end cleandoc) starts a block of the text it hands on: its
        handle_endtag adds a blank line before the element only when the text
        before it ends in a newline, so it does when that text is empty or
        ends in one."""
        before = next((part for part in reversed(self.cleandoc[:-2]) if part), "")
        return not before or before.endswith("\n")

    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):
        block = (not self.inraw and tag in self.block_level_tags and tag not in self.empty_tags
                 and self.get_state(tag, {k: k if v is None else v for k, v in attrs}) == "block")
        was = self.inraw
        self.starting = True
        try:
            super().handle_starttag(tag, attrs)
        finally:
            self.starting = False
        if self.inraw and not was:
            self.opened = self.getpos()[0]
        if self.mdstack and self.outer is None:
            self.outer = self.getpos()[0]
        if block and self.whole_line(self.get_starttag_text()):
            self.edges.add(self.getpos()[0])
        if self.span is None and self.mdstate and self.mdstate[-1] != "block":
            self.span = (self.getpos()[0], len(self.mdstack))
        self.note_top()

    def handle_endtag(self, tag):
        outer = (not self.starting and not self.inraw and tag in self.block_level_tags
                 and tag in self.mdstack)
        super().handle_endtag(tag)
        if self.outer is not None and not self.mdstack:
            if not self.stashed_in_block():
                end = len(self.lines) if self.ending else self.getpos()[0]
                self.raw.update(range(self.outer, end + 1))
            self.outer = None
        if outer and not self.mdstack and self.whole_line(self.get_endtag_text(tag)):
            self.edges.add(self.getpos()[0])
        if self.span is not None and len(self.mdstack) < self.span[1]:
            self.inline.update(range(self.span[0], self.getpos()[0] + 1))
            self.span = None
        if self.opened is not None and not self.inraw and not self.mdstack:
            self.raw.update(range(self.opened, self.getpos()[0] + 1))
            self.opened = None
        self.note_top()

    def handle_empty_tag(self, data, is_block):
        alone = (is_block and not self.inraw and (self.mdstack or not self.intail)
                 and self.at_line_start())
        super().handle_empty_tag(data, is_block)
        if alone:
            line = self.getpos()[0]
            self.raw.update(range(line, line + data.count("\n") + 1))

    def close(self):
        self.ending = True
        super().close()
        if self.opened is not None:
            self.raw.update(range(self.opened, len(self.lines) + 1))
            self.opened = None

    def item_lines(self):
        """The lines in items (see the class), from tops: a line takes the state
        left by the last tag before it."""
        found, item, k = set(), False, 0
        for n in range(1, len(self.lines) + 1):
            while k < len(self.tops) and self.tops[k][0] < n:
                item = self.tops[k][1]
                k += 1
            if item:
                found.add(n)
        return found


def markdown_edges(lines, md):
    """Where md_in_html splits lines into raw HTML and Markdown (MarkdownEdges):
    (edges, inline, raw, items), found on a scratch Markdown instance with md's
    block-level tags and list item tags, in one pass of md_in_html's parser."""
    scratch = markdown.Markdown()
    scratch.block_level_elements = list(md.block_level_elements)
    parser = MarkdownEdges(scratch)
    parser.lines = lines
    indent = md.parser.blockprocessors["indent"]
    parser.item_types = set(indent.ITEM_TYPES) | set(indent.LIST_TYPES)
    parser.feed("\n".join(lines))
    parser.close()
    return parser.edges, parser.inline, parser.raw, parser.item_lines()


class FenceSpans(InlineProcessor):
    """Python-Markdown's backtick pattern, except that a code span starting a
    line with three or more backticks and running over lines, with no other
    backtick on its first line, stays as text: it is a fence Markdown didn't
    take for one (a fence's info string holds no backtick), and the stray fence
    check finds it there. Wraps the pattern it replaces, whatever its version
    does."""

    def __init__(self, backtick):
        super().__init__(backtick.pattern, backtick.md)
        self.backtick = backtick
        self.compiled_re = backtick.getCompiledRegExp()

    def handleMatch(self, m, data):
        el, start, end = self.backtick.handleMatch(m, data)
        if el is not None and start is not None:
            span = data[start:end]
            info = span.split("\n", 1)[0].lstrip("`")
            if (span.startswith("```") and "\n" in span and "`" not in info
                    and not data[data.rfind("\n", 0, start) + 1:start].strip()):
                return AtomicString(span), start, end
        return el, start, end


def stray_fences(doc, source):
    """Where a page's HTML (doc) shows a fence as text: each line whose text
    outside comments, <pre> and <code> is a fence (STRAY_FENCE_RE) and reads
    the same as a line of the page's Markdown (source) less its indent, quote
    marks and list marker. Returns (line in doc, the line's text, those lines
    of the Markdown)."""
    src = [SOURCE_PREFIX_RE.sub("", line).strip() for line in source.splitlines()]
    # Each comment, <pre> and <code> becomes a letter and its newlines, so the
    # lines keep their numbers and text after it doesn't start a line.
    text = CODE_RE.sub(lambda m: "x" + "\n" * m.group(0).count("\n"), doc)
    text = re.sub(r"<[^>]*>", lambda m: "\n" * m.group(0).count("\n"), text)
    found = []
    for n, line in enumerate(html.unescape(text).split("\n"), 1):
        said = line.strip()
        lines = [k for k, s in enumerate(src, 1) if s == said] if STRAY_FENCE_RE.fullmatch(said) else []
        if lines:
            found.append((n, said, lines))
    return found


def nested_pre(doc):
    """The lines of a page's HTML (doc) where a <pre> starts inside another
    <pre>, its tags read as the per-tag check reads them (TAG_RE), so a comment
    or a quoted attribute value holds none. No page the build means to write
    has one: it is a code block set inside another."""
    depth, found = 0, []
    for t in TAG_RE.finditer(doc):
        m = re.match(r"<(/?)pre(?=[ \t\n\f\r/>])", t.group(0), re.I)
        if not m:
            continue
        if m.group(1):
            depth = max(depth - 1, 0)
        else:
            if depth:
                found.append(doc.count("\n", 0, t.start()) + 1)
            depth += 1
    return found


def site_files(out):
    """Every file and symlink under out, as paths relative to it."""
    found = set()
    for d, dirs, files in os.walk(out):
        for name in files + [x for x in dirs if os.path.islink(os.path.join(d, x))]:
            found.add(os.path.relpath(os.path.join(d, name), out))
    return found


def prepare_out(out):
    """Make out an empty directory, clearing it only if it is a site this script built."""
    if out == ROOT / "site":
        pass
    elif out.is_relative_to(ROOT):
        raise BuildError(f"--out {out}: inside the repo; only site/ may be built there")
    elif ROOT.is_relative_to(out):
        raise BuildError(f"--out {out}: holds the repo; not clearing it")
    if out.exists() or out.is_symlink():
        if not out.is_dir():
            raise BuildError(f"--out {out}: not a directory")
        if any(out.iterdir()):
            manifest = out / MANIFEST
            if not manifest.is_file():
                raise BuildError(f"--out {out}: not empty and not a site this build wrote (no {MANIFEST}); "
                                 "not clearing it")
            listed = set(manifest.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()) | {MANIFEST}
            extra = sorted(site_files(out) - listed)
            if extra:
                raise BuildError(f"--out {out}: holds files the build didn't write ({', '.join(extra[:5])}"
                                 f"{', …' if len(extra) > 5 else ''}); not clearing it")
            shutil.rmtree(out)
    out.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    # Claim the directory before writing anything else into it.
    (out / MANIFEST).write_text("", encoding="utf-8")


def write_manifest(out):
    files = sorted(site_files(out) - {MANIFEST})
    (out / MANIFEST).write_text("".join(f + "\n" for f in files), encoding="utf-8")


class Page:
    def __init__(self, src):
        self.src = src
        self.rel = src.relative_to(ROOT)
        self.section = self.rel.parts[0] if len(self.rel.parts) > 1 else ""
        self.out = self.rel.with_suffix(".html")
        md = markdown.Markdown(extensions=["meta"])
        md.convert(src.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        meta = {k: " ".join(v) for k, v in md.Meta.items()}
        self.checked = meta.get("checked", "")
        self.title = meta.get("title", "")
        if not self.title:
            m = re.search(r"^# (.+)$", src.read_text(encoding="utf-8"), re.M)
            self.title = m.group(1).strip() if m else src.stem
        self.is_index = src.name == "index.md"
        self.standalone = not self.section and not self.is_index
        self.published = bool(self.checked) or self.is_index or self.standalone


def collect_pages():
    pages = [Page(ROOT / "index.md")]
    for folder in TOP_PAGES:
        pages.append(Page(ROOT / folder / "index.md"))
    for section in SECTIONS:
        pages.append(Page(ROOT / section / "index.md"))
        for src in sorted((ROOT / section).glob("*.md")):
            if src.name != "index.md":
                pages.append(Page(src))
    for name in STANDALONE:
        if (ROOT / name).is_file():
            pages.append(Page(ROOT / name))
    return pages


def section_pages(pages, section):
    """The section's pages other than its index, in the order every list of
    them (the index lists, the menu, the pager) shows them."""
    return [p for p in pages if p.section == section and not p.is_index]


def section_list(pages, section, here):
    items = []
    for p in section_pages(pages, section):
        if p.published:
            href = os.path.relpath(p.out, here.out.parent)
            items.append(f'<li><a href="{html.escape(href)}">{html.escape(p.title)}</a></li>')
        else:
            items.append(f'<li class="soon">{html.escape(p.title)} <span>(not written yet)</span></li>')
    return "<ol>\n" + "\n".join(items) + "\n</ol>"


def cli_help(cli):
    out = run([cli, "--help"])
    start = out.find("Usage:")
    if start < 0:
        raise BuildError("blockwright-cli --help printed no Usage line")
    out = out[start:]
    # The usage line names the binary it was run as (blockwright-cli-dev).
    out = re.sub(r"^Usage: \S+", "Usage: blockwright-cli", out, count=1)
    return "<pre class=\"help\">" + html.escape(out.rstrip()) + "</pre>"


def app_timeout():
    """APP_TIMEOUT, or the seconds $BW_DOCS_APP_TIMEOUT names."""
    asked = os.environ.get("BW_DOCS_APP_TIMEOUT")
    if asked is None:
        return APP_TIMEOUT
    try:
        seconds = float(asked)
    except ValueError:
        seconds = 0
    if not 0 < seconds < float("inf"):
        raise BuildError(f"BW_DOCS_APP_TIMEOUT: {asked!r} isn't a number of seconds above 0")
    return seconds


def run_app(app, timeout, *args):
    """The app's stdout for args, run with no display. The app sets
    QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen itself for --list-actions; setting it here too
    does no harm."""
    env = dict(os.environ, QT_QPA_PLATFORM="offscreen")
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([app, *args], capture_output=True, text=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace", env=env,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=timeout)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        raise BuildError(f"{app} {' '.join(args)} didn't finish in {timeout:g} s")
    except OSError as e:
        raise BuildError(f"{app} {' '.join(args)} couldn't run: {e.strerror}")
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise BuildError(f"{app} {' '.join(args)} exited {r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()}")
    return r.stdout


KEY_RE = re.compile(r"^((?:(?:Ctrl|Shift|Alt|Meta)\+)*)(.+)$")


def kbd(key):
    """A key in portable text (Ctrl+Shift+B, Ctrl++) as <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>B</kbd>."""
    mods, last = KEY_RE.match(key).groups()
    return "+".join(f"<kbd>{html.escape(k)}</kbd>" for k in mods.split("+")[:-1] + [last])


def does(action):
    """What an action does, as the app says it: its status tip, else its tooltip
    when that says more than its name."""
    text = action["statusTip"].strip()
    if not text:
        lines = action["toolTip"].strip().splitlines()
        # Qt's own tooltip is the menu text, keys after a tab ("Delete\tDel");
        # a tool's starts with its name and key: "Keyboard (Ctrl+K)"
        if lines:
            lines[0] = lines[0].split("\t")[0].strip()
        if lines and (lines[0] == action["text"] or re.fullmatch(re.escape(action["text"]) + r" \(.*\)", lines[0])):
            lines = lines[1:]
        text = " ".join(line.strip() for line in lines if line.strip())
    if action.get("offeredWhen"):
        text = (text + " " if text else "") + f"Offered only {action['offeredWhen']}."
    return text


def shortcuts(app, cli_version, timeout):
    """The tables <!-- generated: shortcuts --> becomes (every action
    `blockwright --list-actions` lists, a table per menu in its order, each
    action's name, default keys and what it does), and the app's version and Qt.
    The app must be from the same Blockwright commit as the CLI (cli_version,
    the first line of its --version)."""
    if not app:
        raise BuildError("<!-- generated: shortcuts --> needs the Blockwright app: none beside the CLI and no "
                         "blockwright-dev or blockwright on PATH; pass --app or set BLOCKWRIGHT_APP")
    version = run_app(app, timeout, "--version").splitlines()
    m = re.fullmatch(r"Qt (\d[\d.]*)", version[1].strip()) if len(version) > 1 else None
    if not version or not m:
        raise BuildError(f"{app} --version names no Qt on its second line; the shortcuts page needs a "
                         "Blockwright whose --version does (#463)")
    name = re.sub(r"^\S+", "Blockwright", version[0].strip(), count=1)
    # "blockwright 0.1.0 (ad55097)" and "blockwright-cli 0.1.0 (ad55097)": the
    # same once the program's name is dropped.
    if version[0].split()[1:] != cli_version.split()[1:]:
        raise BuildError(f"{app} is {version[0].strip()} but the CLI is {cli_version.strip()}; the shortcuts page "
                         "needs the app from the same Blockwright commit as the CLI")
    out = run_app(app, timeout, "--list-actions")
    try:
        actions = json.loads(out)
        groups = {}
        for a in actions:
            groups.setdefault(a["menu"], []).append(a)
            a["text"], a["shortcuts"], a["statusTip"], a["toolTip"]
    except (ValueError, TypeError, KeyError) as e:
        raise BuildError(f"{app} --list-actions printed no action list ({e}): {out.strip()[:200]}")
    if not actions:
        raise BuildError(f"{app} --list-actions listed no actions")
    parts = [f"<p>These are the keys {html.escape(name)} starts with on Qt {m.group(1)}.</p>"]
    if any(a.get("standardKey") for a in actions):
        parts.append("<p>Keys marked <em>(standard)</em> are the platform's standard keys for that action: the "
                     "table gives the ones Qt gives a Linux desktop, and another platform, or another version "
                     "of Qt, can give the action other keys.</p>")
        # Qt gives some standard actions a key of its own (Undo, F14) that
        # some keyboards have; a key with a modifier is made of ordinary keys.
        own = dict.fromkeys(k for a in actions if a.get("standardKey") for k in a["shortcuts"]
                            if not KEY_RE.match(k).group(1))
        if own:
            keys = [kbd(k) for k in own]
            listed = keys[0] if len(keys) == 1 else ", ".join(keys[:-1]) + " and " + keys[-1]
            parts.append(f"<p>{listed} {'is a key' if len(keys) == 1 else 'are keys'} some keyboards have.</p>")
    ids = set()
    for menu, rows in groups.items():
        slug = "keys-" + (re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", "-", menu.lower()).strip("-") or "other")
        n, base = 2, slug
        while slug in ids:
            slug, n = f"{base}-{n}", n + 1
        ids.add(slug)
        parts.append(f'<h3 id="{slug}">{html.escape(menu)}</h3>')
        parts.append('<table class="keys">\n<thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th><th>What it does</th></tr>'
                     "</thead>\n<tbody>")
        for a in rows:
            keys = list(a["shortcuts"]) + [k for k in a.get("hint", "").split(", ") if k]
            cell = ", ".join(kbd(k) for k in keys)
            if keys and a.get("standardKey"):
                cell += " <em>(standard)</em>"
            parts.append(f"<tr><td>{html.escape(a['text'])}</td><td>{cell}</td>"
                         f"<td>{html.escape(does(a))}</td></tr>")
        parts.append("</tbody>\n</table>")
    return "\n".join(parts), f"{name}, Qt {m.group(1)}"


def slug(text):
    return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", "-", text.lower()).strip("-")


def plan_statuses():
    """{(section, slug): status} from PLAN.md: every table with a Status column
    and a Tutorial (tutorials) or Page (manual) column, the status cut to its
    leading words (before the first comma, colon, semicolon or bracket)."""
    try:
        text = (ROOT / "PLAN.md").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError as e:
        raise BuildError(f"<!-- generated: checklist --> needs PLAN.md: {e.strerror}")
    found = {}
    header = None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if not line.startswith("|"):
            header = None
            continue
        cells = [c.strip() for c in re.split(r"(?<!\\)\|", line.strip())[1:-1]]
        if header is None:
            header = cells
            continue
        if all(re.fullmatch(r":?-+:?", c) for c in cells):
            continue
        row = dict(zip(header, cells))
        for column, section in (("Tutorial", "tutorials"), ("Page", "manual")):
            if column in row and "Status" in row:
                found[(section, slug(row[column]))] = re.split(r"[,:;(]", row["Status"], maxsplit=1)[0].strip()
    return found


def inline_code(text):
    """text escaped, with `code` spans as <code>."""
    return re.sub(r"`([^`]+)`", r"<code>\1</code>", html.escape(text, quote=False))


def same_commit(a, b):
    """Whether a and b name one commit: the same hex, or one a prefix of the other
    at 7 digits or more. A build from a changed tree ("7e06a97-dirty") is no
    commit, so no page was checked against it."""
    if not (re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]+", a) and re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]+", b)):
        return False
    return a == b or (min(len(a), len(b)) >= 7 and (a.startswith(b) or b.startswith(a)))


def checklist(pages, here, cli_version):
    """The tables <!-- generated: checklist --> becomes on here: every tutorial
    and manual page, with its PLAN.md status, the commit its Checked:
    names and whether that is the CLI's (cli_version, the first line of its
    --version)."""
    m = COMMIT_RE.search(cli_version.strip())
    if not m:
        raise BuildError(f"<!-- generated: checklist -->: the CLI's version names no commit: {cli_version.strip()}")
    commit = m.group(1)
    name = re.sub(r"^\S+", "Blockwright", cli_version.strip(), count=1)
    statuses = plan_statuses()
    counts = {"ready": 0, "behind": 0, "not written": 0}
    tables = []
    for section, title in SECTIONS.items():
        rows = []
        for p in section_pages(pages, section):
            status = statuses.get((section, slug(re.sub(r"^\d+-", "", p.src.stem))), "")
            if p.published:
                href = os.path.relpath(p.out, here.out.parent)
                cell = f'<a href="{html.escape(href)}">{html.escape(p.title)}</a>'
                cm = COMMIT_RE.search(p.checked)
                checked = f"<code>{html.escape(cm.group(1))}</code>" if cm else html.escape(p.checked)
                ready = "ready" if cm and same_commit(cm.group(1), commit) else "behind"
            else:
                cell, checked, ready = html.escape(p.title), "", "not written"
            counts[ready] += 1
            rows.append(f"<tr><td>{cell}</td><td>{inline_code(status)}</td><td>{checked}</td><td>{ready}</td></tr>")
        tables.append(f'<h3 id="checklist-{section}">{title}</h3>\n<table class="checklist">\n<thead><tr><th>Page</th>'
                      "<th>Status</th><th>Checked against</th><th>Ready</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n"
                      + "\n".join(rows) + "\n</tbody>\n</table>")
    total = sum(counts.values())
    summary = (f"<p>{html.escape(name)}: {counts['ready']} of {total} pages ready, {counts['behind']} behind, "
               f"{counts['not written']} not written.</p>\n"
               f"<p>A page is ready when it is written and was last checked against <code>{html.escape(commit)}</code>. "
               "A page checked against another commit is behind: its steps and options have to be checked again "
               "against this one.</p>")
    return summary + "\n" + "\n".join(tables)


def within(url, base):
    """Whether url is base or under it: base itself, or base followed by a /, a
    ? or a #, so https://git.pumpin.online.example.test isn't the forge."""
    base = base.rstrip("/")
    return url == base or url.startswith((base + "/", base + "?", base + "#"))


def forge_ends(rest):
    """Whether the text after the forge's address in text (FORGE_TEXT_RE's run)
    ends the address under within()'s rule for links, read as written and
    again as a reader sees it (entities decoded, up to a space; a < there came
    from an entity and is a character the reader sees). It ends
    the address when it starts with a /, a ? or a #, or when, after one
    sentence-ending . or , at most, it goes on with no letter, digit, mark or
    _ . , - : @ % ~ (a longer host name or a port), with what a URL parser drops
    from a host name removed, full-width forms folded (NFKC) and an ideographic
    full stop read as a dot; and not when what the reader sees has an @ before
    its first /, ? or # (a user part)."""
    def ends(text):
        if text.startswith(("/", "?", "#")):
            return True
        text = text[1:] if text.startswith((".", ",")) else text
        return not (re.match(r"[\w.,:@~%-]", text) or text and unicodedata.category(text[0]).startswith("M"))

    seen = re.split(SPACE, html.unescape(rest), maxsplit=1)[0]
    host = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", IGNORED_RE.sub("", seen)).replace("\u3002", ".")
    return ends(rest) and ends(host) and "@" not in re.split(r"[/?#]", seen, maxsplit=1)[0]


# A relative address the build lets through: ASCII letters, digits and
# / . _ ~ % - #, not starting with /, so it can name no scheme, host or
# backslash form (empty is the page itself).
SAFE_RELATIVE_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:[A-Za-z0-9._~%-][A-Za-z0-9/._~%-]*)?(?:#[A-Za-z0-9._~%:-]*)?$")
MAILTO_RE = re.compile(r"^mailto:[^\x00-\x20\x7f\\]+$", re.I)
# What a URL parser trims from both ends of an address: C0 controls and space.
C0_SPACE = "".join(map(chr, range(0x21)))


def site_url(how, url):
    """url, which --forge-url or --mirror-url gave, if it is an http or https
    address with a host (and only a port and path after it)."""
    port = re.match(r"^https?://[^/:]+:(\d+)", url)
    host = re.match(r"^https?://([^/:]*)", url).group(1) if URL_RE.match(url) else ""
    last = host.rsplit(".", 1)[-1].lower()
    # A host whose last label is a number (all digits, or 0x and hex digits)
    # is an IPv4 address to a URL parser: only four dotted numbers 0-255 are one.
    numeric = bool(re.fullmatch(r"\d+|0x[0-9a-f]*", last))
    if numeric and not re.fullmatch(r"(?:(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4]\d|1\d\d|[1-9]?\d)\.){3}(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4]\d|1\d\d|[1-9]?\d)",
                                    host):
        host = ""
    if not URL_RE.match(url) or not host or (port and not 0 < int(port.group(1)) < 65536):
        raise BuildError(f"{how}: {url!r} isn't an http:// or https:// address with a host (and an optional port "
                         "and path, no query or fragment)")
    return url


def stylesheet_errors(css, rel="theme/site.css"):
    """Why the stylesheet css could load something, one line per reason; none
    when it uses only CSS_AT_RULES and CSS_FUNCTIONS and no backslash. The text
    is read whole, strings and comments included, so a name in either counts."""
    errors = []
    if "\\" in css:
        errors.append(f"{rel}: a backslash, which can spell url( as a CSS escape; the stylesheet uses none")
    for name in sorted({m.group(1).lower() for m in CSS_AT_RE.finditer(css)} - CSS_AT_RULES):
        errors.append(f"{rel}: @{name}: an at-rule the site doesn't use (the stylesheet loads nothing; it may use "
                      f"{', '.join('@' + a for a in sorted(CSS_AT_RULES))})")
    for name in sorted({m.group(1).lower() for m in CSS_FUNCTION_RE.finditer(css)} - CSS_FUNCTIONS):
        errors.append(f"{rel}: {name}(): a CSS function the site doesn't use (the stylesheet loads nothing; it "
                      f"may use {', '.join(f + '()' for f in sorted(CSS_FUNCTIONS))})")
    return errors


def page_root(page):
    """The way from page up to the site's root: "" or "../" and so on."""
    up = os.path.relpath(".", page.out.parent)
    return "" if up == "." else up + "/"


def is_home(page):
    return not page.section and page.is_index


class Builder:
    def __init__(self, out, cli, app=None, forge=FORGE, mirror=None):
        self.out = out
        self.cli = cli
        self.app = app
        self.forge = forge.rstrip("/")
        self.mirror = mirror
        self.app_used = None
        self.rendered = {}
        self.errors = []
        self.pages = collect_pages()
        self.by_out = {p.out: p for p in self.pages}
        self.template = Template((ROOT / "theme" / "page.html").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        self.version = run([cli, "--version"]).splitlines()[0].replace("blockwright-cli-dev", "blockwright-cli")
        self.app_timeout = app_timeout()

    def render_sample(self, ans, scale):
        """Draw ans (a path from ROOT to an .ans or .bwp) at scale; returns the
        PNG's site path and size. The PNG keeps the sample's whole name
        (final.ans-2x.png), so final.ans and final.bwp in one folder each get
        their own."""
        key = (ans, scale)
        if key in self.rendered:
            return self.rendered[key]
        src = ROOT / ans
        if not src.is_file():
            raise BuildError(f"no sample {ans}")
        png = ans.with_name(f"{ans.name}-{scale}x.png")
        dest = self.out / png
        dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        run([self.cli, "render", "--scale", str(scale), str(src), str(dest)])
        shutil.copyfile(src, self.out / ans)
        self.rendered[key] = (png, png_size(dest))
        return self.rendered[key]

    def fix_images(self, page, body):
        def img(m):
            attrs = m.group(1)
            quoted = re.search(r'(?<![\w-])src[ \t\n\f\r]*=[ \t\n\f\r]*"([^"]+)"', attrs, re.I)
            if not quoted:
                raise BuildError(f"{page.rel}: <img {attrs.strip()[:60]}>: an image whose src isn't in double quotes; "
                                 "write it as Markdown or as src=\"…\"")
            src = html.unescape(quoted.group(1))
            if EXTERNAL_RE.match(src):
                return m.group(0)
            kind = os.path.splitext(unquote(src.partition("#")[0]))[1]
            if kind in IMAGE_TYPES:
                self.copy_asset(page, src)
                return m.group(0)
            if kind not in SAMPLE_TYPES:
                raise BuildError(f"{page.rel}: {src}: not a picture the build can show; an image is a sample "
                                 f"({', '.join(SAMPLE_TYPES)}, drawn by blockwright-cli render) or "
                                 f"{', '.join(IMAGE_TYPES)}")
            title = re.search(r'title="([^"]*)"', attrs)
            scale = 1
            if title:
                sm = SCALE_RE.match(title.group(1))
                if not sm:
                    raise BuildError(f"{page.rel}: {src}: the title names a scale, 1x to 8x, not {title.group(1)!r}")
                scale = int(sm.group(1))
            ans = in_repo(page.rel.parent / unquote(src))
            if ans is None:
                raise BuildError(f"{page.rel}: {src}: outside the repo")
            why = refused(ans)
            if why:
                raise BuildError(f"{page.rel}: {src}: {why}")
            png, (w, h) = self.render_sample(ans, scale)
            alt = re.search(r'alt="([^"]*)"', attrs)
            alt = alt.group(1) if alt else ""
            href = os.path.relpath(ans, page.out.parent)
            pngref = os.path.relpath(png, page.out.parent)
            return (f'<a class="ans" href="{html.escape(href)}"><img src="{html.escape(pngref)}" '
                    f'alt="{alt}" width="{w}" height="{h}"></a>')

        return re.sub(r"<img[ \t\n\f\r]([^>]*)/?>", img, body, flags=re.I)

    def copy_asset(self, page, ref):
        """Copy the repo file a relative link or image names into the site."""
        path = unquote(ref.partition("#")[0])
        if not path:
            return
        target = in_repo(page.rel.parent / path)
        if target is None:
            self.errors.append(f"{page.rel}: {ref}: outside the repo")
        elif refused(target):
            self.errors.append(f"{page.rel}: {ref}: {refused(target)}")
        elif not (ROOT / target).is_file():
            self.errors.append(f"{page.rel}: {ref}: no such file")
        elif (self.out / target).exists():
            return  # a page, a rendered sample or an asset already copied
        else:
            (self.out / target).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            shutil.copyfile(ROOT / target, self.out / target)

    def fix_links(self, page, body):
        def link(m):
            href = html.unescape(m.group(1))
            if EXTERNAL_RE.match(href) or href.startswith("#"):
                return m.group(0)
            path, _, frag = href.partition("#")
            if path.endswith((".md", ".html")):
                target = in_repo(page.rel.parent / unquote(path))
                tp = self.by_out.get(target.with_suffix(".html")) if target else None
                if tp is None:
                    self.errors.append(f"{page.rel}: link to {href}: no such page")
                elif not tp.published:
                    self.errors.append(f"{page.rel}: link to {href}: that page isn't written yet")
                if path.endswith(".md"):
                    path = path[:-3] + ".html"
            else:
                self.copy_asset(page, href)
            return 'href="' + html.escape(path + ("#" + frag if frag else "")) + '"'

        return re.sub(r'href="([^"]+)"', link, body)

    def fix_external(self, page, doc):
        """Puts the forge's address (--forge-url) in place of FORGE in every
        href and src of the page (the template's included), fails an address
        that isn't the forge, the mirror, the licence, a mailto: or a plain
        relative path, and fails a tag or attribute the site doesn't use. In
        text between tags, code and <pre> text included, the forge's address is
        moved too; text is never checked as a link."""
        def fail(why):
            self.errors.append(f"{page.rel}: {why}")

        # The template's <link>: the one stylesheet a page may name.
        own_css = page_root(page) + "site.css"

        def address(raw):
            # As a browser's URL parser reads it: tabs and newlines dropped
            # anywhere, C0 controls and spaces trimmed from both ends. One that
            # needs either is refused rather than read.
            url = re.sub(r"[\t\n\r]", "", raw).strip(C0_SPACE)
            if url != raw:
                fail(f"{raw!r}: an address with control characters or spaces at its ends")
                return raw
            if within(url, FORGE):
                url = self.forge + url[len(FORGE):]
            if within(url, self.forge) or url in ALLOWED_EXTERNAL or bool(self.mirror and within(url, self.mirror)):
                return url
            if SAFE_RELATIVE_RE.match(url) or MAILTO_RE.match(url):
                return url
            if ABSOLUTE_RE.match(url):
                fail(f"{url}: a link out of the site; pages link only to the forge ({self.forge}), the mirror and "
                     "the licence")
            else:
                fail(f"{url!r}: an address the build can't read; a page's own addresses are relative paths of "
                     "letters, digits and / . _ ~ % - with an optional #fragment")
            return url

        def tag(m):
            text = m.group(0)
            if text == "<!--":
                fail("a comment that isn't closed")
                return text
            if text.startswith(("<!", "<?")) or re.match(r"</(?![A-Za-z])", text) and text.endswith(">"):
                return text  # a comment, a doctype or a bogus comment: nothing in it loads
            if text in ("<", "</"):
                if doc[m.end():m.end() + 1].isalpha():
                    fail(f"a tag the build can't read: {doc[m.start():m.start() + 60]!r}")
                return text  # a < that starts no tag
            name = re.match(r"</?[^ \t\n\f\r/>]*", text).group(0)
            tagname = name.lstrip("</").lower()
            if name.startswith("</"):
                if ATTR_RE.search(text[len(name):]):
                    fail(f"</{tagname} ...>: an end tag with attributes")
                return text
            if tagname not in TAGS:
                fail(f"<{tagname}>: a tag the site doesn't use")
                return text
            allowed = ATTRS["*"] | ATTRS.get(tagname, set())

            def attr(a):
                key = a.group(2).lower()
                value = None if a.group(3) is None else html.unescape(
                    next(g for g in (a.group(4), a.group(5), a.group(6)) if g is not None))
                if not (key in allowed or key.startswith("aria-")):
                    fail(f"<{tagname} {key}>: an attribute the site doesn't use")
                elif key == "style" and not STYLE_RE.match(value or ""):
                    fail(f"<{tagname} style={value!r}>: a style the site doesn't use (a column's text-align only)")
                elif tagname == "link" and key == "rel" and (value or "").lower() != "stylesheet":
                    fail(f"<link rel={value!r}>: a link the site doesn't use (its own stylesheet only)")
                elif tagname == "link" and key == "href" and not SAFE_RELATIVE_RE.match(value or ""):
                    fail(f"<link href={value!r}>: a stylesheet from outside the site")
                elif tagname == "link" and key == "href" and value != own_css:
                    fail(f"<link href={value!r}>: a stylesheet other than the site's own ({own_css}); only "
                         "theme/site.css is checked for what it loads")
                elif tagname == "img" and key == "src" and (value or "").lower().partition("#")[0].endswith(SAMPLE_TYPES):
                    fail(f"<img src={value!r}>: a sample the image pass didn't draw")
                elif key in ADDRESS_ATTRS and value is not None:
                    new = address(value)
                    # Written back double-quoted, so the resolve check reads it.
                    if new != value or a.group(4) is None:
                        return f'{a.group(1)}{a.group(2)}{a.group(3)}"{html.escape(new)}"'
                return a.group(0)

            return name + ATTR_RE.sub(attr, text[len(name):])

        def in_text(gap):
            # The forge's address moved, so a command copied from a page names
            # the same forge as its links. --forge-url holds no < > & or quote
            # (URL_RE), so it can't start a tag here.
            return FORGE_TEXT_RE.sub(lambda m: self.forge if forge_ends(m.group(1)) else m.group(0), gap)

        # Only real tags are checked: code and <pre> text has its < escaped. A
        # comment or a bogus comment links to nothing and is left as it is.
        out, at = [], 0
        for m in TAG_RE.finditer(doc):
            out += [in_text(doc[at:m.start()]), tag(m)]
            at = m.end()
        return "".join(out + [in_text(doc[at:])])

    def neighbours(self, page):
        if not page.section or page.is_index:
            return ""
        same = [p for p in section_pages(self.pages, page.section) if p.published]
        i = same.index(page)
        parts = []
        if i > 0:
            parts.append(f'<a rel="prev" href="{same[i-1].out.name}">← {html.escape(same[i-1].title)}</a>')
        if i + 1 < len(same):
            parts.append(f'<a rel="next" href="{same[i+1].out.name}">{html.escape(same[i+1].title)} →</a>')
        return '<nav class="pager">' + " ".join(parts) + "</nav>" if parts else ""

    def menu(self, page):
        """The site menu every page carries: the home link, then each section's
        index and its pages in order, stubs dimmed and unlinked as in the index
        lists. The page itself is marked aria-current, its section "here"."""
        def link(p, text, cls=""):
            href = os.path.relpath(p.out, page.out.parent)
            attrs = f' class="{cls}"' if cls else ""
            if p is page:
                attrs += ' aria-current="page"'
            return f'<a{attrs} href="{html.escape(href)}">{html.escape(text)}</a>'

        home = next(p for p in self.pages if not p.section and p.is_index)
        parts = []
        for folder, name in TOP_PAGES.items():
            top = next(p for p in self.pages if p.section == folder and p.is_index)
            parts.append(f'<div class="top">{link(top, name)}</div>')
        for section, name in SECTIONS.items():
            index = next(p for p in self.pages if p.section == section and p.is_index)
            items = []
            for p in section_pages(self.pages, section):
                if p.published:
                    items.append(f"<li>{link(p, p.title)}</li>")
                else:
                    items.append(f'<li class="soon" title="Not written yet">{html.escape(p.title)}</li>')
            here = " here" if page.section == section else ""
            parts.append(f'<div class="part{here}">\n{link(index, name, "part-name")}\n<ol>\n'
                         + "\n".join(items) + "\n</ol>\n</div>")
        return ('<nav class="side" aria-label="Site">\n' + link(home, SITE_NAME.upper(), "home") + "\n"
                '<details class="menu">\n<summary>Menu</summary>\n<div class="parts">\n'
                + "\n".join(parts) + "\n</div>\n</details>\n</nav>")

    def build_page(self, page):
        text = page.src.read_text(encoding="utf-8")

        def replace(kind, name):
            if kind == "generated":
                if name == "cli-help":
                    return cli_help(self.cli)
                if name == "shortcuts":
                    table, self.app_used = shortcuts(self.app, self.version, self.app_timeout)
                    return table
                if name == "checklist":
                    return checklist(self.pages, page, self.version)
                raise BuildError(unknown_marker(page, f"<!-- generated: {name} -->"))
            if name not in SECTIONS:
                raise BuildError(unknown_marker(page, f"<!-- list: {name} -->"))
            return section_list(self.pages, name, page)

        md = markdown.Markdown(extensions=["meta", "extra", "toc", "sane_lists"],
                               extension_configs={"toc": {"permalink": False}})
        md.preprocessors.register(Markers(md, replace), "blockwright_markers", MARKER_PRIORITY)
        md.preprocessors.register(NestedFences(md), "blockwright_nested_fences", NESTED_FENCE_PRIORITY)
        md.inlinePatterns.register(FenceSpans(md.inlinePatterns["backtick"]), "backtick", BACKTICK_PRIORITY)
        body = md.convert(text)
        left = LEFT_MARKER_RE.search(body)
        if left and left.group(0) not in MARKERS:
            raise BuildError(unknown_marker(page, left.group(0)))
        if left:
            raise BuildError(f"{page.rel}: a <!-- generated: --> or <!-- list: --> marker was left as a "
                             "comment; put it at the start of a line of its own (not indented, not in a list "
                             "item or blockquote), outside code")
        body = self.fix_images(page, body)
        body = self.fix_links(page, body)
        root = page_root(page)
        checked = ""
        if page.checked:
            checked = f'<p class="checked">Describes {html.escape(page.checked)}.</p>'
        title = SITE_NAME if is_home(page) else f"{page.title} · {SITE_NAME}"
        robots = "noindex" if page.standalone else ROBOTS
        robots = f'<meta name="robots" content="{html.escape(robots)}">\n' if robots else ""
        mirror = ""
        if self.mirror:
            mirror = f'<br>\nAlso at <a href="{html.escape(self.mirror)}">{html.escape(self.mirror)}</a>'
        doc = self.template.substitute(
            title=html.escape(title),
            body_class=' class="home-page"' if is_home(page) else "",
            robots=robots,
            root=root,
            body=body,
            checked=checked,
            pager=self.neighbours(page),
            nav=self.menu(page),
            mirror=mirror,
        )
        doc = self.fix_external(page, doc)
        src = [line.strip() for line in text.splitlines()]
        for said in md.preprocessors["blockwright_nested_fences"].refused:
            lines = [k for k, s in enumerate(src, 1) if s == said]
            where = f" ({'line' if len(lines) == 1 else 'lines'} {', '.join(map(str, lines))} of {page.rel})" \
                if 0 < len(lines) <= 3 else ""
            self.errors.append(f"{page.rel}: a code fence in a list straight inside an <li>, <ol> or <ul> marked "
                               f"as Markdown{where}: {said!r}; Python-Markdown puts it beside the list's item, "
                               "outside every <li>; write the list in Markdown outside that element")
        for n, said, lines in stray_fences(doc, text):
            where = f" ({'line' if len(lines) == 1 else 'lines'} {', '.join(map(str, lines))} of {page.rel})" \
                if 0 < len(lines) <= 3 else ""
            self.errors.append(f"{page.rel}: a code fence shown as text at line {n} of {page.out}{where}: {said!r}; "
                               "a fence goes at the left margin, or in a list item at the item's content indent "
                               "(four spaces a level), and closes with the same fence at the same indent")
        dest = self.out / page.out
        dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        dest.write_text(doc, encoding="utf-8")

    def check_refs(self):
        """Every relative href and src in the built site resolves to a file in
        it, and every #fragment to an id on the page it names."""
        ids = {}

        def ids_of(f):
            if f not in ids:
                text = f.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
                ids[f] = {html.unescape(i) for i in re.findall(r'\bid="([^"]*)"', text)}
            return ids[f]

        out = self.out.resolve()
        for f in sorted(out.rglob("*.html")):
            text = f.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
            for ref in re.findall(r'(?:href|src)="([^"]+)"', text):
                ref = html.unescape(ref)
                if EXTERNAL_RE.match(ref):
                    continue
                path, _, frag = ref.partition("#")
                target = (f.parent / unquote(path)).resolve() if path else f
                if not target.is_relative_to(out) or not target.is_file():
                    self.errors.append(f"{f.relative_to(out)}: {ref} doesn't resolve")
                elif frag and target.suffix == ".html" and unquote(frag) not in ids_of(target):
                    self.errors.append(f"{f.relative_to(out)}: {ref}: no #{frag} on that page")

    def check_pre(self):
        """No built page holds a <pre> inside another <pre>."""
        out = self.out.resolve()
        for f in sorted(out.rglob("*.html")):
            lines = nested_pre(f.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            if lines:
                self.errors.append(f"{f.relative_to(out)}: a <pre> inside another <pre> at "
                                   f"{'line' if len(lines) == 1 else 'lines'} {', '.join(map(str, lines))}: "
                                   "a code block was put inside another")

    def build(self):
        prepare_out(self.out)
        try:
            shutil.copyfile(ROOT / "theme" / "site.css", self.out / "site.css")
            self.errors += stylesheet_errors((ROOT / "theme" / "site.css").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            (self.out / "robots.txt").write_text(ROBOTS_TXT, encoding="utf-8")
            for page in self.pages:
                if page.published:
                    self.build_page(page)
            self.check_refs()
            self.check_pre()
        finally:
            write_manifest(self.out)
        if self.errors:
            raise BuildError("\n".join(self.errors))
        n = sum(p.published for p in self.pages)
        app = f", shortcuts from {self.app_used}" if self.app_used else ""
        print(f"build: {n} pages, {len(self.rendered)} pictures, {self.version}{app}, into {self.out}")


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="Build the docs site into site/.")
    ap.add_argument("--out", type=Path, default=ROOT / "site", help="output directory (default: site/)")
    ap.add_argument("--cli", help="the blockwright-cli to render with (default: $BLOCKWRIGHT_CLI, "
                    "blockwright-cli-dev, blockwright-cli)")
    ap.add_argument("--app", help="the Blockwright app whose --list-actions makes the shortcuts page (default: "
                    "$BLOCKWRIGHT_APP, the app beside the CLI, blockwright-dev, blockwright)")
    ap.add_argument("--forge-url", default=FORGE, help=f"the forge's address in every forge and download link "
                    f"(default: {FORGE})")
    ap.add_argument("--mirror-url", help="the site's other address, named in a line of every page's footer "
                    "(default: none, and no line)")
    args = ap.parse_args()
    try:
        forge = site_url("--forge-url", args.forge_url)
        mirror = site_url("--mirror-url", args.mirror_url) if args.mirror_url is not None else None
        cli = find_cli(args.cli)
        Builder(args.out.resolve(), cli, find_app(args.app, cli), forge, mirror).build()
    except BuildError as e:
        sys.exit(f"build: {e}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
